3.\",\"logprobs\":[],\"sequence_number\":43}\n\nevent: response.content_part.done\ndata: {\"type\":\"response.content_part.done\",\"item_id\":\"msg_kaeso_msg01gWpepASbE5pFasXfFjGPVf_1\",\"output_index\":1,\"content_index\":0,\"part\":{\"type\":\"output_text\",\"annotations\":[],\"logprobs\":[],\"text\":\"Yes. Assume there are only finitely many such primes p1, ..., pk and set N = 4·p1·…·pk − 1. N ≡ 3 (mod 4), so at least one prime factor q of N is ≡ 3 (mod 4), because products of primes ≡ 1 (mod 4) are ≡ 1 (mod 4). But q cannot equal any pi, since each pi leaves remainder −1 when dividing N. This contradiction shows there are infinitely many primes p with p mod 4 = 3.\"},\"sequence_number\":44}\n\nevent: response.output_item.done\ndata: {\"type\":\"response.output_item.done\",\"output_index\":1,\"item\":{\"id\":\"msg_kaeso_msg01gWpepASbE5pFasXfFjGPVf_1\",\"type\":\"message\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"output_text\",\"annotations\":[],\"logprobs\":[],\"text\":\"Yes. Assume there are only finitely many such primes p1, ..., pk and set N = 4·p1·…·pk − 1. N ≡ 3 (mod 4), so at least one prime factor q of N is ≡ 3 (mod 4), because products of primes ≡ 1 (mod 4) are ≡ 1 (mod 4). But q cannot equal any pi, since each pi leaves remainder −1 when dividing N. This contradiction shows there are infinitely many primes p with p mod 4 = 3.\"}],\"role\":\"assistant\"},\"sequence_number\":45}\n\nevent: response.completed\ndata: {\"type\":\"response.completed\",\"response\":{\"id\":\"msg_01gWpepASbE5pFasXfFjGPVf\",\"object\":\"response\",\"created_at\":1760000000,\"status\":\"completed\",\"background\":false,\"completed_at\":1760000000,\"error\":null,\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":null,\"max_output_tokens\":null,\"max_tool_calls\":null,\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":null,\"prompt_cache_key\":null,\"reasoning\":{\"effort\":null,\"summary\":null},\"safety_identifier\":null,\"service_tier\":\"default\",\"store\":false,\"temperature\":1.0,\"text\":{\"format\":{\"type\":\"text\"}},\"tool_choice\":\"auto\",\"tools\":[],\"top_logprobs\":0,\"top_p\":1.0,\"truncation\":\"disabled\",\"user\":null,\"metadata\":{},\"model\":\"claude-sonnet-4-6\",\"output\":[{\"id\":\"rs_kaeso_msg01gWpepASbE5pFasXfFjGPVf_0\",\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"summary\":[{\"type\":\"summary_text\",\"text\":\"Let me recall the classic Euclid-style argument adapted to primes of the form 4k+3.\\n\\nSuppose finitely many: p1, ..., pk. Let N = 4(p1...pk) - 1. N is congruent to 3 mod 4. A product of primes that are all 1 mod 4 is 1 mod 4, so N has a prime factor q that is 3 mod 4. q cannot be any pi because N leaves remainder -1 on division by each pi. Contradiction.\"}],\"encrypted_content\":\"kpp:reasoning:v1:\"},{\"id\":\"msg_kaeso_msg01gWpepASbE5pFasXfFjGPVf_1\",\"type\":\"message\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"output_text\",\"annotations\":[],\"logprobs\":[],\"text\":\"Yes. Assume there are only finitely many such primes p1, ..., pk and set N = 4·p1·…·pk − 1. N ≡ 3 (mod 4), so at least one prime factor q of N is ≡ 3 (mod 4), because products of primes ≡ 1 (mod 4) are ≡ 1 (mod 4). But q cannot equal any pi, since each pi leaves remainder −1 when dividing N. This contradiction shows there are infinitely many primes p with p mod 4 = 3.\"}],\"role\":\"assistant\"}],\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":51,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":0,\"cache_write_tokens\":0},\"output_tokens\":612,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":287},\"total_tokens\":663}},\"sequence_number\":46}\n\n",
